==========================================================================
LGPL-2.1-or-later: FFmpeg
==========================================================================
Flower decodes audio with FFmpeg (libavformat, libavcodec, libavutil,
libswresample, https://ffmpeg.org), through FFAudio.NET, a small façade
under the Apache License 2.0 (https://github.com/yanos/FFAudio.NET). The
FFmpeg in every build Flower distributes is built under the LGPL: without
--enable-gpl or --enable-nonfree. Which FFmpeg a running copy holds is shown
in its About window (Settings on a phone), and FFmpeg reports its own
configure line and licence through FFAudio.NET's FFmpegBuild.

How FFmpeg is linked, and how to put a different FFmpeg in its place:

  Windows. FFmpeg ships as its own DLLs (avformat-*.dll, avcodec-*.dll,
  avutil-*.dll, swresample-*.dll) beside ffaudio.dll in the install folder,
  built from source by FFAudio.NET's native/windows/build-ffmpeg.sh. Replace
  them with an ABI-compatible build of your own.

  macOS, Linux, iOS, Android and the server. FFmpeg is linked statically
  into the façade, which is itself a separate shared library inside the
  app: libffaudio.dylib in Flower.app/Contents/MonoBundle (macOS),
  libffaudio.so in the AppImage (Linux) and the APK (Android),
  ffaudio.framework in the app (iOS), and
  /app/runtimes/linux-x64/native/libffaudio.so in the server's Docker
  image. To use a modified FFmpeg, rebuild that library from
  FFAudio.NET's source with your FFmpeg - its
  native/build-all.sh, with the FFmpeg version and component list in
  native/codec-set.sh - and replace the one in the app. FFAudio.NET's
  version is FFAudioPackageVersion in Flower's Directory.Build.props, and
  its repository has a tag for every version. On a phone the changed app
  has to be signed again, with your own key.

The complete corresponding FFmpeg source, on every platform, is FFmpeg's
release of the version FFAudio.NET's codec-set.sh names, unmodified, from
https://ffmpeg.org/releases/, configured as that platform's FFAudio.NET
build script says - and, for as long as Flower distributes a build
containing it, on request from the Flower project through
https://github.com/yanos/Flower/issues.

